{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nThe current reconciler has typed `MutationDirection` and `MutationAction`, but `Mutation.payload` remains `Mapping[str, Any]`. Outbound typed mutations are translated back into the legacy batch dictionary, two CREATE shapes coexist, and string/monkeypatch consumers make deletion non-local. ADR 0103 already owns retry, coordinator, and observe-before-replay policy; this story must not redesign those decisions.\n\nThis is the architecture prerequisite for the typed cutover. It completes RESEARCH and any required ADR lifecycle before dependent implementation begins.\n\n## Approach\n\n- Enumerate every valid `(direction, action)` pair, producer, consumer, serialization/manifest field, compatibility re-export, string import, standalone-loaded test module, and transport boundary. Use semantic references plus string search.\n- Define the smallest discriminated payload union that makes invalid field/action combinations unrepresentable while preserving necessary provenance and canonical comparison.\n- Decide ownership of payload validation, canonical serialization, adapter projection, follow-ons/suppression, binding identity, and provider-neutral outcomes.\n- Specify expand/compare/cut/delete sequencing, rollback, and exact legacy deletion targets. Production dual-send is forbidden.\n- Reconcile the design with ADRs 0004, 0026–0029, 0083, 0092, and 0103. If a new structural decision is required, complete the architecture-decision RESEARCH -> critique -> DRAFT -> HOLD -> MERGE lifecycle before closing this story.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/` — mutation producers, planners, coordinator, adapters, manifests, and compatibility surfaces.\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/` — consumer census and existing contract evidence.\n- `docs/adr/` — one atomic decision record only if accepted ADRs do not already decide the representation boundary.\n\n## Testing\n\nThis story produces architecture evidence rather than production behavior. Verify the consumer census with both Serena semantic references and repository text search; verify the direction/action matrix against the enum allowlist and every planner/adapter route; run plan review on the resulting implementation stories after the ADR disposition is settled.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A checked-in or ticket-recorded census covers every mutation producer/consumer and all string, dynamic-load, monkeypatch, manifest, and transport consumers; an empty semantic result is confirmed by text search.\n- [ ] Every valid direction/action pair has a named payload contract with required/optional fields and validation rules; no proposed contract relies on one option-heavy generic action payload.\n- [ ] The design states which current canonical bytes/hashes are compatibility requirements and which normalized representation is comparison-only.\n- [ ] Retry/idempotency/fuse ownership remains with ADR 0103's coordinator; no adapter or payload type introduces a second retry owner.\n- [ ] Shadow comparison is side-effect-free, and live comparison uses isolated Jira targets/instances rather than dual-sending to one issue.\n- [ ] The migration has explicit entry/exit criteria and names `_mutation_to_batch_dict`, legacy batch dispatch, duplicate CREATE handling, obsolete re-exports, and compatibility tests as deletion targets.\n- [ ] Any required ADR is merged with explicit approval before dependent code work; otherwise the evidence records why accepted ADRs already decide the shape."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788026772753086215,"uuid":"5faf9287-f7f1-4cbf-8ea1-2450ac70ee98"}